ement (spawn count, wall clock, or peak concurrent children) recorded in the completion event; (4) full regression suite green after the change (in-flight runs are not passed). REFERENCE: task-router tests/conftest.py (shared_seed_registry/seed_registry_copy) and tests/test_circuit_hardening.py are the worked examples to copy from.", "ts": "2026-09-19T20:15:17Z", "foreman_note": "load-hygiene audit dispatch", "worktree": "/home/kara/worktrees/coding-hermes-boardctl-BT-039", "branch": "wt/BT-039"}
 {"id": "DF-BOARDCTL-1", "title": "Burndown chart never renders: template reads d.burndown.days but the payload's burndown series has no days key (open only)", "status": "complete", "priority": "P1", "reasoning": "MEASURED 2026-09-20 (dogfood tick boardctl-dogfood-2026-09-20-04-33-31, render/serve surface).\nREPRO: boardctl -C <repo> render -o r.html --json r.json on any board, then open the report and look at the Burndown panel.\nEVIDENCE: the payload's derived.burndown is {\"window\":[...],\"open\":[17 ints]} while derived.burnup is {\"window\":[...],\"days\":[17 dates],\"cum\":[17 ints]}. template.go:714 and :725 both pass days: d.burndown.days || [] into chartLines; chartLines (template.go:388) returns emptyChart(opts.empty) when days.length === 0, so the burndown panel always shows the 'no tasks yet' placeholder and discards the open series.\nPROVEN IN-BROWSER on the rendered boardctl report (42 tasks, 17 burndown points): {burndown_has_days_key:false, burndown_data_points:17, chartLines_BURNDOWN_returns_empty:true, burnup_has_days_key:true, chartLines_BURNUP_returns_empty:false}. Reproduced on a fresh bunker box too (grep 'burndown\"' for a days key: 0 hits vs 1 for burnup).\nIMPACT: one of the two headline charts is blank/honest-looking-empty on EVERY report ever generated; 'no tasks yet' shows on a board with 42 tasks. Verified live on boardctl, crier, and a 3-board serve upload.\nWHY TESTS ARE GREEN: derive_test.go asserts lengths and values of Burndown.Open, and load_test.go:151 asserts a non-nil empty struct; nothing asserts the JSON shape the template consumes. The gap is a template-contract gap, not a derive bug.\nFIX DIRECTION: either add Days to BurndownSeries (derive.go:38) populated from the same window burndown already computes (mirroring BurnupSeries at derive.go:45), or change template.go:714/725 to read window. Add a test that renders the HTML and asserts the burndown panel is not the empty placeholder on a board with open tasks.", "created_at": "2026-09-20T04:49:22Z", "updated_at": "2026-09-20T05:36:45Z", "worker_status": "pending", "detail": {"fingerprint": "1ab83b1cc28099499f10b6956740c68e08f015c7450a265a29f6d2f9abfcd25f"}, "ts": null, "complexity": 3, "foreman_note": "wave dispatch tick boardctl-2026-09-20-04-54-08", "worktree": "/home/kara/worktrees/coding-hermes-boardctl-DF-BOARDCTL-1", "branch": "wt/DF-BOARDCTL-1", "commit_hash": "238fa0e", "guard_result": "PASS", "ci_result": "GREEN", "worker_summary": "template now reads d.burndown.window; render test proves window data in output; merged+pushed, verdict fce860ed", "sessions": ["20260920_001144_d874ac"]}
-{"id": "DF-BOARDCTL-2", "title": "serve accumulates every upload in memory for the life of the process: re-uploading the same boards duplicates them in the report, /api/boards and the compare view", "status": "pending", "priority": "P2", "reasoning": "MEASURED 2026-09-20 (dogfood run, serve surface).\nREPRO: boardctl serve -C <repo> in a browser; upload the SAME two-board zip three times.\nEVIDENCE: GET /api/boards returns [{boardctl},{boardctl},{b2}] after upload 1 (the -C board appears twice before any upload at all), then the name counts grow 2->3 (b2) and 2->4 (boardctl) after two more identical uploads. The rendered report's compare table then lists boardctl TWICE side by side and the report banner reads 'Loaded boardctl, boardctl, b2 with 30 parse warnings'. Root cause: cmd/boardctl/serve.go:366 appends every upload session to s.sessions and currentBoards() (serve.go:201-207) concatenates ALL sessions plus cBoards on every request; nothing dedupes by board dir/slug and no route resets the set.\nIMPACT: on a long-lived server a user's second upload silently makes every report ambiguous (which 'boardctl' row is the new one?), the compare view diffs a board against itself, and memory grows with upload size x upload count. There is also no documented way to clear state short of killing the process. NOTE: the -C board being listed twice even on the first, clean request may be a separate off-by-one in how the -C board is registered alongside the upload - worth checking while fixing.\nFIX DIRECTION: key loaded boards by resolved dir (replace on re-upload), and/or add an explicit reset (button + route) and document the lifetime in the README serve section. Decide and document whether -C should appear once when it is also uploaded.", "created_at": "2026-09-20T04:49:30Z", "updated_at": "2026-09-20T04:49:30Z", "worker_status": "pending", "detail": {"fingerprint": "7a7f981f84356e0777d1987a2da4ed200ff5915cc6710c1a5ac7e249996de7ad"}, "ts": null, "complexity": 3}
+{"id": "DF-BOARDCTL-2", "title": "serve accumulates every upload in memory for the life of the process: re-uploading the same boards duplicates them in the report, /api/boards and the compare view", "status": "complete", "priority": "P2", "reasoning": "MEASURED 2026-09-20 (dogfood run, serve surface).\nREPRO: boardctl serve -C <repo> in a browser; upload the SAME two-board zip three times.\nEVIDENCE: GET /api/boards returns [{boardctl},{boardctl},{b2}] after upload 1 (the -C board appears twice before any upload at all), then the name counts grow 2->3 (b2) and 2->4 (boardctl) after two more identical uploads. The rendered report's compare table then lists boardctl TWICE side by side and the report banner reads 'Loaded boardctl, boardctl, b2 with 30 parse warnings'. Root cause: cmd/boardctl/serve.go:366 appends every upload session to s.sessions and currentBoards() (serve.go:201-207) concatenates ALL sessions plus cBoards on every request; nothing dedupes by board dir/slug and no route resets the set.\nIMPACT: on a long-lived server a user's second upload silently makes every report ambiguous (which 'boardctl' row is the new one?), the compare view diffs a board against itself, and memory grows with upload size x upload count. There is also no documented way to clear state short of killing the process. NOTE: the -C board being listed twice even on the first, clean request may be a separate off-by-one in how the -C board is registered alongside the upload - worth checking while fixing.\nFIX DIRECTION: key loaded boards by resolved dir (replace on re-upload), and/or add an explicit reset (button + route) and document the lifetime in the README serve section. Decide and document whether -C should appear once when it is also uploaded.", "created_at": "2026-09-20T04:49:30Z", "updated_at": "2026-09-20T06:08:40Z", "worker_status": "pending", "detail": {"fingerprint": "7a7f981f84356e0777d1987a2da4ed200ff5915cc6710c1a5ac7e249996de7ad"}, "ts": null, "complexity": 3, "foreman_note": "follow-up dispatch after wave A merge", "worktree": "/home/kara/worktrees/coding-hermes-boardctl-DF-BOARDCTL-2", "branch": "wt/DF-BOARDCTL-2", "commit_hash": "ba0890c", "guard_result": "PASS", "ci_result": "GREEN", "worker_summary": "currentBoards flattened cBoards+sessions; now replaces same-identity boards on upload; 377-line httptest suite; merged+pushed 79a1012", "sessions": ["8368a4"]}
 {"id": "DF-BOARDCTL-3", "title": "Report parser rejects the colon-less UTC-offset dialect (-0500) that boardctl's OWN timestamp detector accepts, silently dropping rows from time-based metrics", "status": "complete", "priority": "P2", "reasoning": "MEASURED 2026-09-20 (dogfood run, render surface).\nREPRO: render a board carrying created_at '2026-09-18T03:47:31-0500' (the dialect internal/board/timefmt.go zoneRe explicitly accepts and preserves: zoneRe = [+-]\\d{2}:?\\d{2}$; see coding-hermes-tools .coding-hermes/board/tasks.jsonl line 1 and 3 more rows).\nEVIDENCE: boardctl -C <board> render --json -> parse_warnings.timestamp_excluded counts those rows and the notes list \"unparseable created_at \\\"2026-09-18T03:47:31-0500\\\" - excluded from time-based metrics\". internal/render/load.go:507 stampLayouts has only two offset forms (Z07:00 with and without a fraction) - none accepts a -0500 offset - so parseStamp (load.go:522) returns ok=false. In the 3-board serve report the b2 board showed 30 excluded timestamps and its velocity collapsed to a single week while its tasks span several.\nSCOPE (measured across 52 fleet boards, 8034 task rows): 4 rows on 1 board carry the literal -NNNN form, BUT the blast radius is larger than those rows because the same gap can silently zero a whole board's time series when the dialect is used for created_at/updated_at.\nWHY IT MATTERS: boardctl ships the inverse rule - the detector accepts and PRESERVES -0500, but its own report parser cannot read it back. A writer/reader round trip inside one tool that loses data is a trust defect, not a cosmetic one. (Verified the writer itself is safe: boardctl create up-converts to +00:00, so the asymmetry is read-side only.)\nFIX DIRECTION: add '2006-01-02T15:04:05.999999999Z0700' and '2006-01-02T15:04:05Z0700' to stampLayouts, and add a table test pairing DetectTSLayout's accepted dialects with parseStamp so the two lists cannot drift apart again.", "created_at": "2026-09-20T04:49:30Z", "updated_at": "2026-09-20T05:36:45Z", "worker_status": "pending", "detail": {"fingerprint": "f9c43b3eb7ffd21e7104a76f2d41ccdf1459efc8b6056c1ab60e7c92be1c2572"}, "ts": null, "complexity": 3, "foreman_note": "wave dispatch tick boardctl-2026-09-20-04-54-08", "worktree": "/home/kara/worktrees/coding-hermes-boardctl-DF-BOARDCTL-3", "branch": "wt/DF-BOARDCTL-3", "commit_hash": "816b211", "guard_result": "PASS", "ci_result": "GREEN", "worker_summary": "colon-less -0500 layouts added to stampLayouts w/ tests; merged+pushed, verdict 81d2730e", "sessions": ["20260920_001208_9efd28"]}
 {"id": "DF-BOARDCTL-4", "title": "Velocity headline silently reports less than the board's own completion count: completions recorded only in events.jsonl are never counted", "status": "complete", "priority": "P2", "reasoning": "MEASURED 2026-09-20 (dogfood run, render surface, crier board).\nEVIDENCE: crier has 391 status=complete rows but derived.velocity totals 199 completions for the whole window - less than HALF the completions the board reports, on the chart captioned 'Are we completing more or fewer tasks per week than last month?'. Breakdown (measured): 192 complete rows have neither a parseable completed_at nor updated_at (data-quality: 182 of them DO carry a task_completed event in events.jsonl, so the completion is recorded - just not on the row); the remaining 199 get their done day from row timestamps only. The events stream has 291 task_completed events, 182 of which belong to rows whose done day is uncomputable.\nRELATED (verified as CORRECT, do not 'fix'): on a row-timestamp-complete board (boardctl itself) velocity DOES fall back to updated_at for a complete row missing completed_at - derive.go:335-338 - so the mechanism exists, it just never consults events.jsonl.\nIMPACT: the 'weekly velocity' chart understates delivered work by 2x on a mature fleet board, and the discrepancy is invisible in the UI - it looks like a quiet week. Cycle time has the same root: crier's cycle block reports n=199, excluded=192.\nFIX DIRECTION: cap velocity at the board's completion count and state the caveat, or count task_completed events for complete rows with no computable done day (and expose the fallback count in the payload so the number is auditable). Whatever is chosen, the chart and derived.complete_count must agree or the difference must be visible.", "created_at": "2026-09-20T04:49:37Z", "updated_at": "2026-09-20T05:36:45Z", "worker_status": "pending", "detail": {"fingerprint": "eae1c21616ad9fd3736694fc47a8ed7b6c77130f68ad6fe65f03f6af9a5fdd06"}, "ts": null, "complexity": 3, "foreman_note": "wave dispatch tick boardctl-2026-09-20-04-54-08", "worktree": "/home/kara/worktrees/coding-hermes-boardctl-DF-BOARDCTL-4", "branch": "wt/DF-BOARDCTL-4", "commit_hash": "3ec5ffd", "guard_result": "PASS", "ci_result": "GREEN", "worker_summary": "event-only completions counted in velocity, deduped by id, RED-proofed; merged+pushed, verdict 48fffce5", "sessions": ["20260920_001226_6b5f9d"]}
 {"id": "DF-BOARDCTL-5", "title": "Data-quality footnote is fleet-global in serve: multi-board reports list another board's no-done ids in every report, including single-board ones", "status": "complete", "priority": "P1", "reasoning": "MEASURED 2026-09-20 (dogfood run, serve surface).\nEVIDENCE: the 3-board serve upload of [boardctl, b2] has payload.completed_no_done_ids = [CHT-007, CHT-008, CHT-023, CHT-026, CHT-027, CHT-046, CHT-050] - those are coding-hermes-tools (b2) row ids. The render footer reads payload.completed_no_done_ids and prints 'completed tasks with no computable completion day (never counted in time series): <ids>' under a 'Data quality' heading (template.go:1321-1324) - it is NOT scoped per board, so a viewer looking at the boardctl board inside that report is shown b2's uncomputable rows as if they were boardctl's. Confirmed the mechanism in code: report.go BuildBoards:80-94 unions noDoneCompletedIDs(d) across ALL boards into one payload-level set, while every other per-board field is namespaced under boards[]. Contrast with single-board render, where the payload-level field happens to be correct because there is only one board.\nIMPACT: an attribution defect in the exact place the report is meant to build trust - the honesty footnote names the wrong board on every multi-board (serve) report, which is the report's flagship mode. On a large fleet upload it degenerates into an unattributable id dump.\nFIX DIRECTION: move completed_no_done_ids under each boards[] entry (or key the payload-level set by board slug/name) and render it in that board's own panel.", "created_at": "2026-09-20T04:49:37Z", "updated_at": "2026-09-20T05:36:45Z", "worker_status": "pending", "detail": {"fingerprint": "bb871826ed9e35d15c90f986e1e7f1edbb25c1e0fc7a384f8bc833bb92cbc198"}, "ts": null, "complexity": 3, "foreman_note": "wave dispatch tick boardctl-2026-09-20-04-54-08", "worktree": "/home/kara/worktrees/coding-hermes-boardctl-DF-BOARDCTL-5", "branch": "wt/DF-BOARDCTL-5", "commit_hash": "a73316b", "guard_result": "PASS", "ci_result": "GREEN", "worker_summary": "footnote scoped per-board via BoardPayload field; merged+pushed, verdict 43a81eea", "sessions": ["20260920_001253_03332b"]}
